artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider singular nonlinear elliptic boundary value problems of the form

    L u + g(u) = f(x, u)  in Ω,    u > 0 in Ω,    u = 0 on ∂Ω,

where Ω ⊂ ℝ^N is a smooth bounded domain, g: (0, ∞) → (0, ∞) is a singular absorption nonlinearity (typically g(s) → +∞ as s → 0^+, e.g. g(s) = s^{-γ} with γ > 0), and f(x, u) is a (sublinear, linear, or superlinear) source term. For the Laplacian L = -Δ and for the p-Laplacian L = -Δ_p, multiplicity results are known: under suitable hypotheses on g and f, the problem admits at least two distinct positive (possibly free-boundary) solutions.

Establish (or disprove) analogous multiplicity-of-positive-solutions results when L is replaced by one of the following classes of diffusion operators:

  (i) a general linear second-order uniformly elliptic operator L u = -∑ a_{ij}(x) ∂_{ij} u + ∑ b_i(x) ∂_i u + c(x) u;
  (ii) fully nonlinear Hamilton–Jacobi type operators, e.g. L u = -Δ u + H(x, ∇u) with H having superlinear gradient growth;
  (iii) Monge–Ampère type operators, e.g. det(D^2 u) appearing as the principal part;
  (iv) the bi-Laplacian Δ^2 (with appropriate Navier or Dirichlet boundary conditions);
  (v) nonlinear elliptic systems with singular absorption couplings;
  (vi) Schrödinger-type systems with singular nonlinearities.

Specifically: identify natural hypotheses on the data (g, f, coefficients) under which the corresponding singular elliptic problem possesses at least two positive solutions (or at least two positive free-boundary solutions), and provide a variational, topological, or sub/supersolution framework that yields such multiplicity in each of the settings (i)–(vi).
